WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:10.920
Here’s a brainteaser: what do a Portuguese
Man O’ War, a coral head, and you

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.620
have in common? …Turns out, you’re all
superorganisms. Sounds pretty cool, right?

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:22.200
A superorganism is basically an organism made
up of many smaller organisms. Think of it

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.210
like a city; all the people who live and work
there have their own lives, but they rely

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:32.800
on each other for things like food, clothing,
and other services. Together, they bring the

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.219
city to life. Now, let’s take a look at
the Man O’ War. If we look closely, we see

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:43.329
tiny units called zooids. Just like the members
of a city, these are each individual animals,

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:49.319
but they are specialized to work together
as one large organism. The coral head is even

00:00:49.319 --> 00:00:55.609
more complex. Scores of algae and bacteria
live inside it. They provide the coral with

00:00:55.609 --> 00:01:00.319
food through photosynthesis, along with essential
nutrients that the coral could not obtain

00:01:00.319 --> 00:01:06.380
on its own. And you are no different. Microbes
living in your body play an essential role

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.009
in keeping you alive and well.
Lactobacillus, Firmicutes, Candida, and HERV.

00:01:09.009 --> 00:01:13.810
These are just a few of the bacteria, fungi,
and viruses that call the human body their

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:20.320
home.  We call this rich ecosystem of non-human cells
microbiome. We get our initial

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.909
stock of microbes from our mothers in the
birth canal, and the population grows over

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:30.990
time. There are so many microbes, in fact,
that their cells outnumber human cells ten

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:37.149
to one. In an adult human, the microbiome
may actually weigh up to five whole pounds.

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:44.600
Most important, however, is the staggering amount
of genetic material contained within it. Our microbial genes,

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:52.820
outnumber our human genes by a factor of about 200.  That means that over
99% of our biochemical functions—everything

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:59.590
that we break down, produce, convert, and
recycle—occur by courtesy of our single-celled

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.229
friends.
And that makes them really important. So important

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:09.800
that human breastmilk has co-evolved to nourish
not only a baby’s developing body, but also

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:16.659
its microbes. Their unique molecular toolkits make enzymes,
vitamins and compounds that fight

00:02:16.659 --> 00:02:22.099
infection and disease. So it should come as
no surprise that when the diversity of our

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:25.269
microbial ecosystem drops, we get sick.

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:25.310
 

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:25.890
 

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:31.599
We still have much to learn, but it’s clear now that our
microbiome plays a fundamental role in human

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:36.760
health and disease.
The Human Microbiome Project launched in 2007

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:43.200
to characterize the microbiome and assess its role
in health and disease. Microbial communities

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.730
are harvested from their natural environments,
like the nose, mouth, skin, or gut, and their

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:55.370
DNA is isolated and sequenced. This information
can tell us what species are present, and

00:02:55.370 --> 00:03:02.120
what functions they are performing.
In the era of precision medicine, we can use this data

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.689
to formulate personalized therapies. For example, individuals with
kidney stones may be given a probiotic of

00:03:07.689 --> 00:03:12.540
Oxalobacter formigines, a gut bacterium that
degrades mineral deposits.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:21.790
Prebiotics, or compounds that selectively stimulate the growth of good microbes, may also become more widely used. Perhaps most exciting,

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:28.379
however, is the potential of endowing our
microbial community with brand-new functions.

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:33.769
Using synthetic biology, scientists have already
engineered viruses that degrade bacterial

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:39.299
blockades, E.coli that seek and destroy tumors,
and microbes that silence the cholera toxin.

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:39.720
Introducing these functions into our own microbial
ecosystems will present many challenges, but

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.569
the rewards are enticing.
As we learn more about the human microbiome,

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:47.150
we will likely come to
a greater appreciation of its role in our

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:51.099
health, development, and behavior.
Our so-called “second genome” will become

00:03:51.099 --> 00:03:56.260
a major factor in clinical
medicine and biomedical research. So learn

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:00.319
to love your marvelous
microbes. Take care of them, and they’ll

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:13.489
take care of you.

